+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered over self-hosted WebSocket connections — the board owns its own connection tier rather than depending on a realtime vendor. This meets the freshness target with low-latency pushes while keeping the board free of provider coupling and recurring vendor fees, in exchange for owning connection capacity and reconnect handling. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
